le\": \"src/api/routes.py\",\n      \"line\": 130,\n      \"description\": \"Error responses leak internal system details via HTTPException detail. Stack traces and database errors are exposed to clients, aiding reconnaissance.\",\n      \"type\": \"Information Disclosure\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/api/websocket.py\",\n      \"line\": 122,\n      \"description\": \"broadcast_error sends error messages to ALL connected WebSocket clients, not just the one that experienced the error. Could leak information between sessions.\",\n      \"type\": \"Information Disclosure\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/api/websocket.py\",\n      \"line\": 97,\n      \"description\": \"No input validation on WebSocket JSON data. query_text is accepted without length limits, sanitization, or content validation. Could enable prompt injection attacks on the LLM.\",\n      \"type\": \"Input Validation\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/api/routes.py\",\n      \"line\": 121,\n      \"description\": \"No rate limiting on API endpoints. An attacker can spam /queries, /feedback, /agents endpoints without throttling, causing DoS or excessive database queries.\",\n      \"type\": \"Denial of Service\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/api/websocket.py\",\n      \"line\": 76,\n      \"description\": \"No rate limiting on WebSocket endpoint. Multiple concurrent connections can be established without limits, enabling connection exhaustion attacks.\",\n      \"type\": \"Denial of Service\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/data/db.py\",\n      \"line\": 19,\n      \"description\": \"Database file path is predictable: data/hr_policy_agent.db. An attacker with file system access could directly access or modify the database.\",\n      \"type\": \"Insecure Storage\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/fetch/engine.py\",\n      \"line\": 54,\n      \"description\": \"Hardcoded User-Agent mimics Chrome browser. While not a direct vulnerability, this could be flagged as suspicious by security tools and bypasses rate limiting based on user-agent detection.\",\n      \"type\": \"Configuration\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/main.py\",\n      \"line\": 27,\n      \"description\": \"No Content-Security-Policy, X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options, or other security headers configured. Application is vulnerable to clickjacking and header-based attacks.\",\n      \"type\": \"Missing Security Headers\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/main.py\",\n      \"line\": 8,\n      \"description\": \"No HTTPS enforcement. FastAPI serves over plain HTTP. API credentials and user data are transmitted unencrypted.\",\n      \"type\": \"Insecure Transport\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"file\": \"src/api/routes.py\",\n      \"line\": 133,\n      \"description\": \"Path traversal risk: query_id parameter is user-controlled and used directly in database queries. While parameterized queries prevent SQL injection, insufficient validation of the format could enable other attacks.\",\n      \"type\": \"Input Validation\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/llm/client.py\",\n      \"line\": 35,\n      \"description\": \"GEMINI_API_KEY is passed via environment variable and configured globally. If process memory is dumped or logs are captured, the API key could be exposed.\",\n      \"type\": \"Credential Management\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/main.py\",\n      \"line\": 43,\n      \"description\": \"Static files served from /static, /css, /js directories. If sensitive files end up in these directories (config files, backup files), they become publicly accessible.\",\n      \"type\": \"Information Disclosure\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"low\",\n      \"file\": \"src/api/routes.py\",\n      \"line\": 95,\n      \"description\": \"Database query count and feedback count are exposed in /health endpoint. This information could be used for reconnaissance or to identify when the system is being used.\",\n      \"type\": \"Information Disclosure\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"riskScore\": 78,\n  \"recommendations\": [\n    \"Implement proper authentication (OAuth2, API keys, JWT) on all endpoints. At minimum, require an API key header or implement role-based access control.\",\n    \"Configure CORS to allow only trusted origins. Replace allow_origins=['*'] with specific domains: allow_origins=['https://trusted-domain.gov.ca']\",\n    \"Add rate limiting to all endpoints using python-ratelimit or similar library. Implement per-IP and per-session limits.\",\n    \"Enforce HTTPS/TLS in production. Use reverse proxy (nginx) or configure FastAPI with SSL certificates.\",\n    \"Add security headers middleware: Content-Security-Policy, X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options, Strict-Transport-Security.\",\n    \"Validate and sanitize all user inputs, especially query_text. Implement input length limits and content filtering.\",\n    \"Remove detailed error messages from API responses. Return generic error messages to clients and log detailed errors server-side only.\",\n    \"Separate error broadcasts per WebSocket session. Never broadcast errors to all connected clients.\",\n    \"Implement proper database file permissions. Store database outside web root and restrict file access to application user only.\",\n    \"Add audit logging for all sensitive operations (feedback export, query access, system modifications).\",\n    \"Consider storing sensitive database files in /var/lib/app/ or similar with restricted permissions (600) instead of relative paths.\",\n    \"Implement input validation middleware to enforce maximum query text length and reject suspicious patterns.\",\n    \"Add request/response logging with proper sanitization of sensitive data.\",\n    \"Implement WebSocket authentication: validate that the client that opened the connection is the same one sending queries.\",\n    \"Move API key and other secrets to .env file or secrets manager (AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault). Never commit secrets to git.\",\n    \"Add CSRF protection (even for JSON APIs) by validating request origins.\",\n    \"Consider implementing rate limiting at reverse proxy level (nginx) for better performance.\",\n    \"Implement database backups with encryption at rest.\",\n    \"Add integrity checks for cached content (validate content_hash in fetch_cache table).\",\n    \"Test for prompt injection vulnerabilities in the LLM client by fuzzing query inputs.\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Key Issues Summary\n\n**Critical (3):**\n1. **No Authentication** - All endpoints are publicly accessible\n2. **Overly Permissive CORS** - Allows requests from any origin\n3. **Data Exposure** - Unprotected feedback export endpoint\n\n**High Priority (5):**\n- Missing security headers\n- No rate limiting (DoS risk)\n- Information disclosure via error messages  \n- Unencrypted HTTP transport\n- Input validation gaps on WebSocket queries\n\n**Next Steps:**\n1. Add authentication (API key or OAuth2) to all endpoints\n2. Restrict CORS to specific trusted origins\n3. Deploy behind HTTPS reverse proxy\n4. Implement rate limiting middleware\n5. Add input validation and sanitization layer\n",
